    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented idempotent owner creation for `POST /api/owners`: when a create request carries an `Idempotency-Key` header that has already been used, the original owner is returned with **200** instead of creating a duplicate (which the existing uniqueness rules would otherwise reject with 409).\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions, I kept each piece in a small single-responsibility unit and reused the existing 200-responder rather than duplicating it:\n\n**New pieces**\n- `rest/idempotency/IdempotencyStore.java` \u2014 a `@Component` holding the key\u2192owner-id mapping (in-memory, thread-safe). The one place that owns \"which key created which owner.\"\n- `rest/function/owner/IdempotencyKey.java` \u2014 a small variable type carrying the (possibly absent) header value between pipeline steps, with an `isPresent()` helper so the header-absent case is handled in one spot.\n- `rest/function/owner/CheckIdempotencyKey.java` \u2014 the new **entry step**. Reads the optional header via `ServerHttpConnection` (returns `null` cleanly when absent, so the many existing keyless POSTs are unaffected), publishes the key, and either takes the `replay` branch (republish the stored owner) or the `proceed` branch into the normal create pipeline.\n- `rest/function/owner/RecordIdempotencyKey.java` \u2014 a side-effect step after `SaveOwner` (id now assigned) that records the key\u2192id mapping; a no-op when no key was supplied.\n\n**Wiring** (`owners.POST.yml`)\n- `checkIdempotency` is now the first step, using `outputs: { replay \u2192 respondExisting, proceed \u2192 validate }`. `respondExisting` reuses the existing `RespondWithOwner` (200 with `OwnerDto`).\n- `recordIdempotency` inserted between `save` and `audit`.\n\nBoth new steps carry `govern: [ transaction ]` to stay within the endpoint's single-commit governance, matching every other step.\n\n**Result:** `AcceptanceTest` (including the new `coreIdempotentRepeat`) passes \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\nOne design note worth surfacing: the store is in-memory (no DB schema change), which satisfies the spec and tests. If you later need idempotency to survive restarts or work across multiple app instances, that store would need to be backed by a shared/persistent layer \u2014 the `IdempotencyStore` interface boundary is where you'd swap that in.",
